  "question": {
    "text": "Which of the following compounds show colour due to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></math> transition?",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "p7yHK",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CuSO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>·</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "kZ19X",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>Cr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>7</mn></msub></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "riekl",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>CrO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "89Ghq",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>KMnO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>The hydrated copper sulphate </p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CuSO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></math> is blue in color. The ligand (water molecules) cause splitting of d – orbitals. We know that, due to this, the process of d – d transition and color transition is facilitated.</p><p>Whereas, on the other hand the anhydrous copper sulphate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CuSO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math><br/> is colourless. In the absence of ligand (water molecules), splitting of d orbitals is not possible. Hence, d – d transition is not possible.<br/>We can understand the above from the following statements.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CuSO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></math> is blue in colour because its oxidation state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></math>, thus it has unpaired electrons in it. So, d –d transition is possible so it exhibits colour where on the other hand the anhydrous copper sulphate complex, d – d transition is not possible that’s why it is colourless.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CuSO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Cu</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>:</mo><mn>3</mn><mo> </mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mn>9</mn></msup><mn>4</mn><mo> </mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></math></p><p>unpaired electron present so it show colour due to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></math> transition.</p>",
    "image": null
  },
